      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Against the <a href="/proform-power-995i-treadmill">Power 995i</a>,
          the 1295i's extra $300 buys a genuinely stronger motor, a steeper
          incline ceiling, a larger screen, and a full year of iFit
          included rather than paid add-on — worth it if you're training
          more seriously than casual walking. Against the
          <a href="/proform-pro-2000-treadmill">Pro 2000</a>, the
          comparison is closer: the Pro 2000 adds two inches of deck width,
          a decline function, and a slightly larger screen for roughly
          another $300. Unless you're specifically tall, want decline
          training, or are chasing the closest possible match to
          NordicTrack's Commercial 1750, the 1295i covers nearly the same
          ground for less.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's worth noting that the 1295i is arguably the best
          demonstration of ProForm's overall pricing philosophy: each step
          up the lineup buys a specific, identifiable upgrade rather than a
          vague "premium" markup. That transparency makes it easier to
          decide which model earns its price for your specific training
          style, which we break down further in our
          <a href="/proform-treadmill">ProForm brand guide</a>.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-black text-amber-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 9v3.75m0 3.75h.007v.008H12v-.008zM21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path>
            </svg>
            Buying Tip
          </p>
          <p class="text-amber-800 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            When comparison shopping online, search both "ProForm Power
            1295i" and "ProForm Smart Power 1295i" — retailers sometimes
            list the same unit under different names with slightly
            different bundled accessories or pricing, and checking both
            listings can turn up a better deal on the identical machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          One more thing worth flagging for anyone cross-shopping outside
          the ProForm family entirely: the 1295i's 3.5 CHP motor and 12%
          incline ceiling put it in the same general performance
          neighborhood as several mid-range machines from competing
          brands that cost noticeably more. The trade-off, as always with
          ProForm relative to those competitors, tends to show up in frame
          gauge and long-term duty rating rather than day-one performance.
          If you're comparing the 1295i against non-ProForm options in the
          same price bracket, our
          <a href="/best-treadmill-for-home-reviews">best treadmills for home guide</a>
          walks through how several of those alternatives stack up on the
          same criteria.
        </p>

        <h2 id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">Who Should Skip This Treadmill</h2>
        <p>
          The 1295i is a strong middle-ground pick, but it isn't the right
          machine for every shopper. If you're over 6'2" or specifically
          want decline training, the extra deck width and decline function
          on the <a href="/proform-pro-2000-treadmill">Pro 2000</a> are
          worth the additional cost. If your training is mostly casual
          walking a few times a week, the 12% incline ceiling and iFit
          bundle are more machine than you need — the
          <a href="/proform-power-995i-treadmill">Power 995i</a> covers
          that use case for less. And if you know for certain you'll never
          pay for an iFit subscription once the included year ends, you're
          paying for touchscreen hardware you won't use at full value; a
          simpler console-based machine from our
          <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews">budget treadmill guide</a>
          may be the smarter buy.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's also worth addressing the naming question one more time in
          the context of buying confidence, since it's the single most
          common point of hesitation we hear from readers about this
          specific model. Dual naming on a single SKU is unusual enough
          that shoppers reasonably wonder if they're being shown two
          different treadmills, or if one name represents an older or
          lesser version of the other. Neither is true here — ProForm and
          its retail partners simply use both names in marketing and
          listings, and the unit that arrives at your door will be
          identical either way, right down to the model number printed on
          the frame itself. If a listing's price or bundled accessories
          differ between the two names, that's a retailer-level difference,
          not a product-level one, and it's worth comparing before you buy
          rather than assuming they're interchangeable on price too.
        </p>

        <h2 id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">Final Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          The ProForm Power 1295i — or Smart Power 1295i, depending on
          where you're shopping — earns its place as the genuine
          middle-ground option in ProForm's lineup. It doesn't feel like a
          compromise between the 995i and Pro 2000 so much as a
          deliberately calibrated option for intermediate runners who want
          real incline training and iFit coaching without paying flagship
          prices or needing the widest possible deck.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you've read this far and you're deciding between all three
          current ProForm models, our advice is simple: match the deck
          width and incline range to your actual training, not your
          aspirational training. If 20 inches of deck width and a 12%
          incline ceiling cover what you'll really do on this machine week
          to week, the 1295i is very likely the smartest dollar-for-dollar
          buy in ProForm's current lineup.
        </p>
