        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the ProForm Power 1295i the same as the ProForm Smart Power 1295i?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Yes. This model is marketed under both names depending on the
              retailer and packaging — you'll see it listed as the
              ProForm Power 1295i and, just as often, the ProForm Smart
              Power 1295i. They refer to the same treadmill with the same
              3.5 CHP motor, 20 x 60-inch deck, and 12-inch touchscreen, so
              don't worry about picking the "wrong" one if you see both
              names while shopping.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does the 1295i compare to the ProForm Power 995i?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The 1295i steps up with a stronger 3.5 CHP motor versus the
              995i's 3.0 CHP, a steeper 12% max incline versus 10%, a
              larger 12-inch touchscreen versus 10 inches, and iFit
              included for the first year rather than merely compatible.
              For roughly $300 more, it's worth it if incline training or
              guided coaching matter to your routine.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does the 1295i compare to the ProForm Pro 2000?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The Pro 2000 adds a wider 22-inch deck versus the 1295i's
              20-inch deck, a slightly stronger 3.6 CHP motor, a larger
              14-inch touchscreen, and a -3% decline function the 1295i
              doesn't have. It costs roughly $300 more. The 1295i is the
              better buy if deck width isn't a dealbreaker for your height
              and stride; the Pro 2000 is worth the upgrade for taller
              runners or anyone who wants the closest thing to
              NordicTrack's Commercial 1750 at a lower price.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the ProForm Power 1295i include iFit?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Yes, it ships with a one-year iFit membership included,
              matching the arrangement on the Pro 2000. Most of the
              touchscreen's standout features — auto-adjusting incline
              during trainer-led runs, the full workout library — require
              an active membership, so budget for the ongoing subscription
              cost once the included year ends.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the 20-inch deck on the 1295i too narrow for running?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              For most runners, no. Our testers up to 6'1" had no issues
              with stride length or width during regular jogging and
              interval sessions. Only our tallest testers, above 6'2",
              noticed the narrower width compared to a 22-inch deck during
              faster full-stride running, which is where the wider Pro
              2000 becomes the better fit.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does assembly take on the ProForm Power 1295i?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Around 60 minutes working alone, or roughly 40 minutes with a
              second person helping lift the console mast into place. It
              sits between the lighter Power 995i and the heavier Pro 2000
              in both weight and assembly time.
            </p>
